Based on over 25 years in computers owning a company for 5 years you see=20
this change in the computing market over time.  When I sold Alpha based=20
systems there was always a bitch about cost.

However, people that needed the compute cycles were more than willing to=20
purchase Alpha over Intel because of what it brought them in total TCO.=20
  More compute cycles, memory and bandwith.

Main problem was Digital at the time, was they never knew how to sell=20
Alpha other than with UNIX.  They tried selling it with MS Windows and=20
never made a dent in the market until OEM's starting selling it in the=20
3D space with a little package called Renderman.  This was big hit with=20
film studios.   Remember the movie Titanic?  Rendered on Alpha to give=20
you a time line. The fastest cpu was a 21064, 275MHz and a system cost=20
about $12,000.00.

The Alpha market started to take off when Digital screwed up with a=20
product call the Multia.  This was a 21066 processor, 166Mhz or 233Mhz.=20
The Multia was Digital's attempt to build a X terminal for Windows NT.=20
It failed and left DEC with 15,000 of these pigs sitting around.

Now Digital needed to get rid of them quick. The plan was to sell them
with Linux and hopefully develop the Linux space.  These Multia/UDB sold=20
for less than $2000.00. That's when Alpha started to take off. I=20
personally sold tons of them. In fact, in a former life I even sold a=20
system or two to David Mosberger.

So I'll agree that when the cost comes down more people will get involved
with the ia64.

BTW: Intel is looking to release a single cpu version of the ia64=20
sometime this year.  When this happens I believe you'll see the market=20
open up.

